---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="01-variables-types"
  lessonSlug="01-first-variable"
  hook="A variable is a name you give a value so you can refer to it later. That is the whole idea — but everything else in this course is built on it."
>
  <h2>Why we need variables</h2>
  <p>
    A computer can compute <code>3 + 4</code> just fine. The trouble starts when
    the same number shows up in five different places. If you change one of them
    and forget the others, the program breaks. A <strong>variable</strong> is a
    name that holds onto a value for you, so you only have to write the value
    once.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    caption="Without a variable · don't do this"
    code={`print("Hi Alice")
print("Alice's age is 18")
print("Happy birthday, Alice!")
# If Alice changes their name, you have to edit all three lines.`}
  />

  <CodePlayground
    caption="With variables · this is the move"
    code={`name = "Alice"
age = 18

print("Hi", name)
print(f"{name}'s age is {age}")
print(f"Happy birthday, {name}!")
# Change name once at the top; all three lines update.`}
  />

  <h2>The assignment statement</h2>
  <p>
    The single most important symbol in this lesson is <code>=</code>. In Python
    it is the <strong>assignment operator</strong>. It does <em>not</em> mean
    "equals" in the math sense — read it as <em>"gets"</em>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>age = 18</code> &nbsp;→&nbsp; "the name <code>age</code> gets the value <code>18</code>"</li>
    <li><code>name = "Alice"</code> &nbsp;→&nbsp; "the name <code>name</code> gets the string <code>"Alice"</code>"</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The name always goes on the <strong>left</strong>, the value on the
    <strong>right</strong>. Reversing them is a syntax error:
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    caption="Try running this — and read the error carefully"
    code={`18 = age`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Read the error message">
    <p>
      You'll see <code>SyntaxError: cannot assign to literal here.</code> Python
      is telling you it expected a name on the left. Errors in Python are usually
      this direct — learn to read them, don't panic at them.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Naming rules</h2>
  <p>A name in Python can use letters, digits, and underscores. There are three rules:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Must <strong>start with a letter</strong> or underscore — not a digit.</li>
    <li>No spaces, no hyphens, no <code>@ # $</code>.</li>
    <li>Cannot be a Python <strong>keyword</strong> like <code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>return</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    By convention in Python, names use <strong>snake_case</strong>:
    <code>first_name</code>, <code>total_cents</code>, <code>is_logged_in</code>.
    Not <code>firstName</code> (that's Java/JavaScript), not <code>FirstName</code>
    (Python reserves that for class names).
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    caption="Which of these names are legal?"
    code={`# Try uncommenting each line one at a time and run.

first_name = "Alice"     # ✓ snake_case
age2 = 18                # ✓ digits allowed after the first character
_private = True          # ✓ underscore allowed

# 2nd_name = "Bob"       # ✗ starts with a digit
# first-name = "Bob"     # ✗ Python reads this as: first MINUS name
# class = "CSC 141"      # ✗ class is a Python keyword
# my name = "Bob"        # ✗ spaces are not allowed

print("All defined names:", first_name, age2, _private)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="= is not ==">
    <p>
      <code>=</code> assigns. <code>==</code> compares. We'll see <code>==</code> in
      Chapter 2 when we get to conditionals. Mixing them up is the most common
      bug in your first month of Python.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Variables can be reassigned</h2>
  <p>
    The "variable" part of the word is literal: the value behind a name can
    change. The new value simply replaces the old one.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`score = 0
print("Start:", score)

score = score + 10     # read right to left: "old score + 10, store back in score"
print("After hit:", score)

score = score + 25
print("After bonus:", score)`}
  />

  <Quiz
    question="After this code runs, what does `print(x)` show?"
    choices={[
      { text: "10",  explain: "Close — that was x's first value. But the next line reassigned it." },
      { text: "20",  correct: true, explain: "Right. The second `x = ...` discards the old 10 and stores 20." },
      { text: "30",  explain: "Python doesn't add the two assignments together; it replaces." },
      { text: "Error — you can't assign to the same name twice", explain: "Reassignment is totally fine in Python. That's why they're called *variables*." },
    ]}
    hint="x = 10  then  x = 20  then  print(x)"
  />

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open this lesson in <strong>WolfCode</strong>: the file <code>01-first-variable.py</code>
    has <code>age = ?</code> waiting for a value. Assign your own age, then run
    the lesson with <em>Ctrl+Enter</em>.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Tests are watching">
    <p>
      The autograder reads three things: that <code>age</code> exists, that
      <code>name</code> exists, and that <code>print</code> mentions both. You'll
      see "3 / 3 tests pass" in the WolfCode bottom dock when you've got it.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
